      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-252697",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose $Y$ is a random variable with support $S = {0, 1, 2, \u2026}$. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observations $(y_{i})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\ge 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequencies $(O_{i})$ | $15$ | $38$ | $29$ | $18$ | $0$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted at the $1%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_{0}:Y\u223cPo(\u03bb)$\n- $H_{1}:Y\u2241Po(\u03bb)$\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W \\ge w) = p$, where $W\u223c\u03c7^{2}(\u03bd)$.\n\n| $\u03bd\u2216p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $6.635$ | $3.841$ | $2.706$ |\n| $2$ | $9.210$ | $5.991$ | $4.605$ |\n| $3$ | $11.345$ | $7.815$ | $6.251$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nRounded to one decimal place, the test statistic is\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe number of degrees of freedom for this test is $\u03bd =$ {{field-2}}.\n\nAt the $1%$ significance level, there is {{field-3}} evidence to reject the null hypothesis.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
