      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-252766",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose $Y$ is a random variable with support $S = {0, 1, 2, 3, \u2026}$. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observations $(y_{i})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\ge 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequencies $(O_{i})$ | $18$ | $14$ | $22$ | $10$ | $16$ |\n| Expected Frequencies $(E_{i})$ | $11.9655$ | $22.7344$ | $21.5977$ | $13.6786$ | $10.0238$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted at the $5%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_{0}:Y\u223cPo(1.9)$\n- $H_{1}:Y\u2241Po(1.9)$\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W \\ge w) = p$, where $W\u223c\u03c7^{2}(\u03bd)$.\n\n| $\u03bd\u2216p$ | $0.010$ | $0.025$ | $0.050$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $11.345$ | $9.348$ | $7.815$ |\n| $4$ | $13.277$ | $11.143$ | $9.488$ |\n| $5$ | $15.086$ | $12.833$ | $11.070$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nRounded to one decimal place, the test statistic is approximately\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe number of degrees of freedom for this test is $\u03bd =$ {{field-2}}.\n\nAt the $5%$ significance level, there is {{field-3}} evidence to reject the null hypothesis.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
